---
import ProjectLayout from "../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Quadruped Robot"
  category="Project | Biomimetic Robotics"
  year="2025"
  description="Biomimetic quadruped robot developed at KM-RoBoTa, focused on lightweight mechanical architecture, trunk design, articulated joints and improved torque-to-weight ratio."
>

  <p slot="intro">
    A biomimetic quadruped robot developed at KM-RoBoTa to explore lightweight
    mechanical architectures, human- and animal-inspired articulation, and
    more efficient structural integration.
  </p>


  <!-- PROJECT SNAPSHOT -->

  <section class="portfolio-hero">

    <div class="portfolio-outcome">

      <span class="portfolio-kicker">
        Outcome
      </span>

      <h2>
        Designing the robot body as part of its intelligence.
      </h2>

      <p>
        The project focused on redesigning the mechanical architecture of a
        biomimetic quadruped robot to reduce mass while increasing structural
        rigidity and improving the torque-to-weight ratio of the system.
      </p>

      <div class="portfolio-metrics">

        <div>
          <strong>-20%</strong>
          <span>Reported mass reduction</span>
        </div>

        <div>
          <strong>23</strong>
          <span>Articulated joints</span>
        </div>

        <div>
          <strong>ROS2</strong>
          <span>Validation environment</span>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>


    <figure class="portfolio-main-visual">

      <img
        src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/quadruped-main.jpg"
        alt="Biomimetic quadruped robot developed at KM-RoBoTa"
      />

      <figcaption>
        Final architecture | Lightweight biomimetic quadruped robot
      </figcaption>

    </figure>

  </section>


  <!-- 01 | CONTEXT -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      01
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Context
      </h2>

      <p>
        The project was developed during my robotics and R&D internship at
        KM-RoBoTa in Lausanne.
      </p>

      <p>
        The objective was to rethink the mechanical architecture of a quadruped
        robot using biomimetic principles rather than relying only on conventional
        rigid robotic structures.
      </p>

      <p>
        The work focused on the body, trunk, rear-leg architecture and articulated
        joints as a complete mechanical system.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 | DESIGN CHALLENGE -->

  <section class="portfolio-visual-section">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        02 | Design challenge
      </span>

      <h2>
        More mobility. Less mass.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="process-grid">

      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/reference-architecture.jpg"
          alt="Initial quadruped robot architecture"
        />

        <figcaption>
          01 | Initial architecture
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/kinematic-study.jpg"
          alt="Quadruped robot kinematic study"
        />

        <figcaption>
          02 | Kinematic and joint study
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/new-architecture.jpg"
          alt="Redesigned quadruped robot architecture"
        />

        <figcaption>
          03 | Redesigned architecture
        </figcaption>

      </figure>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 | OBJECTIVES -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      03
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Engineering objectives
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Reduce overall moving mass
        </li>

        <li>
          Increase structural rigidity
        </li>

        <li>
          Improve torque-to-weight ratio
        </li>

        <li>
          Increase useful range of motion
        </li>

        <li>
          Develop more biomimetic joint behaviour
        </li>

        <li>
          Integrate a more functional trunk architecture
        </li>

        <li>
          Maintain realistic manufacturability and assembly
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 | MY CONTRIBUTION -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      04
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        My contribution
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Designed a new quadruped mechanical architecture
        </li>

        <li>
          Developed a redesigned trunk structure
        </li>

        <li>
          Worked on rear-leg integration
        </li>

        <li>
          Designed and refined articulated joint systems
        </li>

        <li>
          Reduced structural mass while increasing rigidity
        </li>

        <li>
          Improved the torque-to-weight characteristics of the robot
        </li>

        <li>
          Supported prototype development and validation
        </li>

        <li>
          Used ROS2-based test environments during validation activities
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 | SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE -->

  <section class="portfolio-system">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        05 | Architecture
      </span>

      <h2>
        The body as a mechanical system.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="system-grid">

      <figure class="system-overview">

        <img
          src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/system-overview.jpg"
          alt="Overall architecture of the quadruped robot"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Overall system | Trunk, limbs and articulated joints
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <div class="subsystem-stack">

        <figure>

          <img
            src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/joint-detail.jpg"
            alt="Detailed quadruped robot joint architecture"
          />

          <figcaption>
            Joint architecture | Mechanical articulation and integration
          </figcaption>

        </figure>


        <div class="subsystem-copy">

          <h3>
            Integrated architecture
          </h3>

          <p>
            Rather than treating the limbs and body as independent assemblies,
            the robot was considered as a complete kinematic chain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Joint geometry, actuator position, mass distribution and neighbouring
            structures all influenced the resulting motion and mechanical efficiency.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 | TRUNK -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      06
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Trunk design
      </h2>

      <p>
        The trunk plays a central role in the mechanical behaviour of a quadruped.
      </p>

      <p>
        It connects the front and rear limbs, carries part of the robot mass and
        directly influences posture, mobility and structural stiffness.
      </p>

      <p>
        My work included developing a new trunk architecture with the objective
        of increasing rigidity while limiting unnecessary mass.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 | JOINT ARCHITECTURE -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      07
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Joint architecture
      </h2>

      <p>
        The complete robot architecture included 23 articulated joints.
      </p>

      <p>
        The joints were designed as part of the complete body architecture,
        taking into account angular travel, mechanical interference, actuator
        integration and the resulting reachable workspace.
      </p>

      <p>
        This approach made it possible to explore more biomimetic forms of
        articulation while maintaining mechanical coherence across the robot.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 08 | MOTION VISUAL -->

  <section class="portfolio-wide-visual">

    <figure>

      <img
        src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/motion-study.jpg"
        alt="Quadruped robot motion and range of motion study"
      />

      <figcaption>
        Motion study | Kinematic configurations and reachable workspace
      </figcaption>

    </figure>

  </section>


  <!-- 09 | LIGHTWEIGHT DESIGN -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      09
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Lightweight design
      </h2>

      <p>
        Reducing moving mass was one of the main engineering objectives.
      </p>

      <p>
        The redesigned architecture achieved a reported mass reduction of
        approximately 20% while increasing structural rigidity.
      </p>

      <p>
        Lower mass also improves the effectiveness of available actuator torque,
        contributing to a better torque-to-weight ratio.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 10 | BIOMIMETIC APPROACH -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      10
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Biomimetic approach
      </h2>

      <p>
        Biological locomotion systems do not rely on isolated rigid mechanisms.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bones, joints, muscles and compliant tissues interact as a complete
        mechanical system.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project explored how some of these principles could inspire a more
        integrated robotic architecture, where useful behaviour emerges partly
        from the body itself.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 11 | DEVELOPMENT PROCESS -->

  <section class="portfolio-visual-section">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        11 | Development
      </span>

      <h2>
        Model. Prototype. Test. Refine.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="process-grid">

      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/cad-development.jpg"
          alt="CAD development of the quadruped robot"
        />

        <figcaption>
          CAD development
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/prototype.jpg"
          alt="Physical prototype of the quadruped robot"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Physical prototype
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/quadruped-robot/test-rig.jpg"
          alt="Quadruped robot validation setup"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Validation | Mechanical and motion testing
        </figcaption>

      </figure>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 12 | ROS2 -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      12
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Validation with ROS2
      </h2>

      <p>
        Validation rigs supported by ROS2 were used during the development process
        to accelerate testing and evaluate mechanical behaviour.
      </p>

      <p>
        Combining physical prototypes with software-supported test environments
        made it possible to iterate more rapidly between mechanical design,
        motion analysis and experimental validation.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 13 | DOCUMENTATION -->

  <section class="portfolio-documents">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        13 | Documentation
      </span>

      <h2>
        Engineering records.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="document-grid">

      <a
        href="/documents/quadruped-robot/design-report.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        class="document-card"
      >

        <div>

          <span class="document-type">
            PDF
          </span>

          <h3>
            Design report
          </h3>

          <p>
            Mechanical architecture, design iterations and project methodology.
          </p>

        </div>

        <span class="document-arrow">
          ↗
        </span>

      </a>


      <a
        href="/documents/quadruped-robot/kinematic-study.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        class="document-card"
      >

        <div>

          <span class="document-type">
            PDF
          </span>

          <h3>
            Kinematic study
          </h3>

          <p>
            Joint architecture, motion analysis and reachable configurations.
          </p>

        </div>

        <span class="document-arrow">
          ↗
        </span>

      </a>


      <a
        href="/documents/quadruped-robot/test-report.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        class="document-card"
      >

        <div>

          <span class="document-type">
            PDF
          </span>

          <h3>
            Test report
          </h3>

          <p>
            Prototype observations, validation results and design conclusions.
          </p>

        </div>

        <span class="document-arrow">
          ↗
        </span>

      </a>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 14 | SKILLS -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      14
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Engineering skills
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Biomimetic robotics
        </li>

        <li>
          Mechanical architecture
        </li>

        <li>
          Kinematic design
        </li>

        <li>
          Lightweight structures
        </li>

        <li>
          Joint design
        </li>

        <li>
          CAD modelling
        </li>

        <li>
          Rapid prototyping
        </li>

        <li>
          ROS2-supported validation
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 15 | LEARNING -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      15
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        What I learned
      </h2>

      <p>
        This project significantly strengthened my interest in embodied intelligence
        and biomimetic robotics.
      </p>

      <p>
        It showed me that robot performance can be improved not only through better
        control algorithms, but also by designing the mechanical architecture to
        naturally provide better motion, lower mass and more efficient load paths.
      </p>

      <p>
        It also reinforced the importance of rapid physical iteration when
        developing complex robotic mechanisms.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>

</ProjectLayout>